data.several-agents-in-one-vm#Several agents:
Several agents can work in one --clone VM, each on a branch of its own in a git worktree of its own, so each agent edits its own copy of the files. Put the worktrees inside the clone, such as in .worktrees/NAME, because the clone’s parent folders in the VM belong to root, and so git worktree add ../NAME fails there. In the VM, in the clone:
echo .worktrees/ >> .git/info/exclude # once, so git status in the clone leaves the worktrees out git worktree add -b agent/api .worktrees/api # a worktree on a new branch, agent/api cd .worktrees/api # then start the agent here What the agents in one VM share:
One repo. Every worktree shares the clone’s repo, so the agents’ branches are all in it. One git fetch devvm on this machine brings every agent’s branch back, such as devvm/agent/api, and you review each one as you would any of the VM’s work (fetching the VM’s work). Before devvm rm deletes the VM, it also checks each worktree inside the clone for uncommitted changes (deleting the VM). Not their dependencies. Install each worktree’s dependencies in that worktree, and never share one node_modules folder between worktrees, because each branch can need different packages, and two agents installing at once can break it. pnpm and bun keep one package store in the VM and hard-link packages from it into each worktree’s node_modules when both are on the same disk, so another worktree costs little download or disk. The VM’s ports, so two servers on the same port collide. Give each worktree’s server a port of its own, such as with PORT=3001. A VM that forwards every port, as new VMs do by default, forwards each of them to this machine’s localhost (ports). The rest of the VM, its network tier and rules, its secrets, and its CPUs, memory and disk, and each agent can read and change every other agent’s worktree. When the agents need different network tiers or rules, secrets or sizes, or when one agent mustn’t reach another’s work, give each agent a folder of its own on this machine, such as a separate clone of the repo, and run devvm --clone in each, so each gets a VM of its own.

data:
devvm supports the Dev Container specification, so a project’s devcontainer.json sets up its VM. When you run devvm in a folder with a .devcontainer/devcontainer.json, or else a .devcontainer.json at the folder’s root, the new VM gets that file’s settings by default, as far as devvm supports them. To make the VM without reading the file, run devvm --no-devcontainer.
The settings devvm supports are the container image to start from, the features devvm has layers for, the ports, the environment and the commands. devvm says in one line which file it read.
{ "image": "mcr.microsoft.com/devcontainers/javascript-node:22", "features": { "ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/docker-in-docker:2": {} }, "forwardPorts": [3000], "remoteEnv": { "PATH": "${containerEnv:PATH}:${containerWorkspaceFolder}/bin" }, "postCreateCommand": "npm ci", "postStartCommand": "npm run db:start" }

data.devvm-space-json#devvm space –json:
devvm space --json prints both tables as one JSON object, as output for scripts describes, and devvm still says on stderr what it says without --json. devvm has a row of the first table each, with sizes in bytes:
Field what, count, size the row’s WHAT, COUNT and SIZE backend the backend that has them, lima, for VMs, images and shared tools, left out for the other rows and for a shared tool’s build when devvm can’t tell which backend it’s for sized whether devvm knows the size, which SIZE shows as - when it doesn’t kept the row’s KEPT gc what devvm gc frees of it now rest the row’s FREED BY retain, retain_frees the retention setting that applies to the row and what it would delete now, both left out when none is on total and gc are the totals row’s SIZE and GC. backends has the second table’s rows, each with its backend, name, path, count, size and sized. unknown names the backends that one of your VMs uses that didn’t answer, and strays has the VMs devvm has no state for, each with its backend, name and the data folder its label names. folder_gone has the VMs whose folder is gone, each with its backend, name and folder, and folder_not_connected has those whose folder is on a disk that isn’t connected, with the same fields. devvm leaves these out when they’re empty.
{ "version": 1, "devvm": [ { "what": "VMs", "backend": "lima", "count": 2, "size": 5368709120, "sized": true, "kept": "only copy", "gc": 0, "rest": "devvm rm" }, { "what": "history", "count": 3, "size": 3145728, "sized": true, "kept": "only copy", "gc": 0, "rest": "gc --history", "retain": "30d", "retain_frees": 1048576 } ], "total": 5371854848, "gc": 0, "backends": [ { "backend": "lima", "name": "download cache", "path": "/Users/you/Library/Caches/lima", "count": 1, "size": 629145600, "sized": true } ] }
